<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">20 November in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">8 events that happened on 20 November, from 284 to 1945.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diocletian.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">284 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,742 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The army proclaims Diocletian Roman emperor</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After Emperor Carus and his son Numerian died on campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, the troops proclaimed the cavalry commander Diocles emperor as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>. He defeated Carus's surviving son Carinus and later created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetrarchy">Tetrarchy</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1194">1194 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">832 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Emperor Henry VI enters Palermo, capital of the Kingdom of Sicily</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> Henry VI, meeting little resistance, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, capital of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, which he claimed through his wife Constance. He was crowned king on 25 December, and the next day Constance gave birth to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vi-holy-roman-emperor">Henry VI, Holy Roman Emperor</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1272">1272 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">754 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Edward I is proclaimed king while returning from crusade</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Four days after Henry III's death, his son Edward was proclaimed king though still abroad. Edward I conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, expelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in 1290 and waged long wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1407">1407 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">619 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">John the Fearless and Louis of Orléans swear a reconciliation</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-duke-of-berry">John, Duke of Berry</a>, secured a vow of solemn reconciliation between his nephews, the rivals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-fearless">John the Fearless</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-duke-of-orleans">Louis I, Duke of Orléans</a>. Three days later Louis was murdered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on Burgundy's orders.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-fearless">John the Fearless</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-duke-of-orleans">Louis I, Duke of Orléans</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1759">1759 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">267 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Hawke defeats the French fleet at Quiberon Bay</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">In a gale off Brittany, Admiral Edward Hawke pursued the French fleet among the rocks and shoals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-quiberon-bay">Quiberon Bay</a> and destroyed or scattered it. The victory ended French plans to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1820">1820 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">206 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">A sperm whale sinks the whaleship Essex</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">In the South Pacific, an enraged sperm whale rammed and sank the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantucket">Nantucket</a> whaleship Essex. Survivors spent about three months in open boats, resorting to cannibalism; the story inspired Moby-Dick.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1917">1917 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">109 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">British tanks attack in mass at Cambrai</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Some 476 British tanks led a surprise attack at Cambrai, breaking through German lines several miles in a day. A German counter-attack soon recovered most of the ground, but the tactics pointed to the future.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1945">1945 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">81 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Nuremberg trials begin</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The International Military Tribunal opened at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> to try 22 senior Nazi leaders for war crimes, crimes against peace and crimes against humanity. The trial laid foundations for international criminal law.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a></div></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 20 November <span class="n">5</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ernest-augustus-elector-of-hanover"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ernest-augustus-elector-of-hanover.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Ernest Augustus, Elector of Hanover</span><span class="tp">1629–1698</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-alexandre-berthier"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-alexandre-berthier.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Louis-Alexandre Berthier</span><span class="tp">1753–1815</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid-laurier"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/wilfrid-laurier.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Wilfrid Laurier</span><span class="tp">1841–1919</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/percy-cox"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/percy-cox.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Percy Cox</span><span class="tp">1864–1937</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/nadine-gordimer"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/nadine-gordimer.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Nadine Gordimer</span><span class="tp">1923–2014</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 20 November <span class="n">4</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/edmund-the-martyr"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/edmund-the-martyr.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Edmund the Martyr</span><span class="tp">d. 869</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/bernward-of-hildesheim"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Bernward of Hildesheim</span><span class="tp">c. 960–1022</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/francisco-franco.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Francisco Franco</span><span class="tp">1892–1975</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ian-smith"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ian-smith.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Ian Smith</span><span class="tp">1919–2007</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
